I wrote the fan review for Joseph Zada to `review-fan.json`. It passes the review schema, its hashes match the request, and every quoted passage appears word for word in the draft. I changed no other files.

I reopened four of the interviews (Interview Magazine, Behind The Blinds, Variety Australia, HERO) and checked Wikipedia and the casting reports. Most quotes hold up. The draft is honest about its promotional sources, the two school-exit accounts and Emily Alyn Lind's "He just gets what he wants."

**Four major findings:**
1. **The opening misrepresents the key quote.** The draft says his answer to "You were more scared after finding out?" was one word, "Yes." The transcript runs on: "Yes. Then the announcement came out a day after…". "More scared" is also Elle Fanning's phrase, not his. Yet the title, meta title, description and main type argument all present it as his own words. It was also said in a Hunger Games promo chat, and in that story winning is where Haymitch's trouble starts.
2. **The Variety interview is misdated.** It was published in February 2025, but it came from a set visit during filming, which began in March 2024. That makes it the one pre-fame source, and it breaks the draft's claim that all sources date from February 2025 on and that nothing exists from before he was 19.
3. **The name-change section leaves out his heritage.** He says himself that his grandmother is Australian Indigenous and Afghan, and the draft never mentions it. It also sets his account against Wikipedia's as if they conflict, when they fit together. The name is read only as seeking security, and never as claiming an identity.
4. **The "avoid the authority, then get their approval" pattern only fits one of its two examples.** He wasn't avoiding the 1955 East of Eden film by choice: he'd planned to watch it, and someone at the callback asked him not to. No approval followed either; he watched it and said "Oh, shit."

**Three minor findings:**
- "Fourteen months apart" is wrong; the two interviews are about eight and a half months apart. The "family time" quote refers to nightly dinners with Mckenna Grace and her mother, not his own family.
- Readers get little role context. He plays Cal Trask, the James Dean role, and says he's only "in the last two episodes," so calling it one of his two biggest performances overstates it. Charlie Roth's story and which characters Fanning and Grace play are also never explained.
- Naming the older brother as Hal is the author's guess, since the interview never names him. The fitting context is also missing: Hal won an American TV series role from an audition, which matches "when he'd come back from America."

I also marked five passages to keep, including the handling of the two school-exit accounts, the confidence-once-peers insight and the "not a whole lot of chill" follow-up. The similarity check found no meaningful reuse. The existing risk flags (contested typing, source uncertainty) carry forward.

None of this overturns the Type 6 reading. Fixes 1, 2 and 4 do weaken the main argument as worded, so the editor should restate it in Zada's own words.
